    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Vikings were Scandinavian raiders, traders and settlers whose voyages between the 790s and the 1060s reached from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a> and from the Arctic to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</li><li>Their raids fell hardest on monasteries and towns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, Ireland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a>, but they also founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, settled Iceland and Normandy, ruled much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and gave a dynasty to the Rus’.</li><li>At home the age saw trading towns, the first kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, after which royal conquest took the place of free-roving raids.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Wood cut with metal blades at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanse-aux-meadows">L’Anse aux Meadows</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> has been dated to the year 1021 by a spike in radiocarbon that a solar storm of 993 left in tree rings: the earliest exact year known for Europeans in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

