    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">793</span><span class="rf-text">Raiders plunder the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a>, the attack that usually marks the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">860</span><span class="rf-text">Rus’ ships attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>; by tradition <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik">Rurik</a> begins to rule at Novgorod two years later</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">865</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-heathen-army">Great Heathen Army</a> lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> defeats it at Edington in 878</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 870–930</span><span class="rf-text">Norse settlers take land in Iceland and found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/althing">Althing</a>, its general assembly, about 930</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">911</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rollo">Rollo</a> receives lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-simple">Charles the Simple</a>, the beginning of Normandy (traditional date)</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 985</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erik-the-red">Erik the Red</a> leads settlers from Iceland to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1000</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leif-erikson">Leif Erikson</a> sails to Vinland; Norse visitors cut wood at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanse-aux-meadows">L’Anse aux Meadows</a> in 1021</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1066</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-hardrada">Harald Hardrada</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> is killed at Stamford Bridge, weeks before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings">Battle of Hastings</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/viking-age"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/viking-age.jpg" alt="Viking Age" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bildsten_fr%C3%A5n_Smiss,_Gotland.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">historical museum, CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The word Viking comes from Old Norse, in which a víkingr was a sea raider and víking an expedition overseas; its deeper origin is disputed, perhaps from vík, a bay, or from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viken">Viken</a>, the land around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslofjord">Oslofjord</a>. The peoples they attacked rarely used it. English and Frankish chroniclers called them Northmen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> or heathens, the Irish called them Gaill, foreigners, and in the east they were Rus’ and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangians">Varangians</a>. Modern historians use “Viking” for the raiders and, more loosely, for the Scandinavians of the period, and date the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> from the attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a> in 793 to 1066.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> before 793 was a land of farms and chieftains, with rich boat graves at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vendel">Vendel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valsgarde">Valsgärde</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and trading places such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribe">Ribe</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, founded in the early 8th century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/staraya-ladoga">Staraya Ladoga</a>, about 753, on the route to the east. The sail, probably adopted in the north only in the 7th or 8th century, now joined oars and keels to make ships that could cross open sea. Raiding across the Baltic had already begun: at Salme on the Estonian island of Saaremaa, two ships held 41 warriors killed in battle about 700–750, men who, by the evidence of their teeth and weapons, came from central Sweden.</p><p>Why the raids began is debated. Historians point to better ships; to the knowledge of rich and poorly defended coasts that trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> had brought; to struggles for power at home that sent defeated or ambitious men abroad; to the lure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and slaves; and perhaps to the advance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Frankish empire</a> to the borders of Denmark. The first raid recorded in England struck Portland in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a>, about 789, when three ships from Hordaland killed the king’s reeve. In 793 raiders plundered the monastery of Lindisfarne, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcuin-of-york">Alcuin of York</a>, the Northumbrian scholar at Charlemagne’s court, wrote to King Æthelred of Northumbria that “never before has such terror appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> as we have now suffered from a pagan race”. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a> was attacked in 795, and from then on raids fell on Ireland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and the coasts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="society">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/oseberg-ship"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oseberg-ship.jpg" alt="Oseberg ship" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/oseberg-ship">Oseberg ship</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Osebergskipet_2016.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Petter Ulleland, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Viking-Age society was built on the farm. Most Scandinavians were free farmers who raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, sheep, pigs, barley and oats, fished, hunted and made iron from bog ore, living in long houses where people and animals often shared one roof. Above them stood chieftains, jarls and kings, whose power rested on land, gifts and loyal warriors; below them were thralls, slaves, many of them captives taken in raids. Free men settled disputes and made decisions at assemblies called things. In Iceland, which had no king, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/althing">Althing</a> met each summer at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thingvellir">Þingvellir</a> from about 930, with a law-speaker who recited the law.</p><p>Women ran the household and its stores, and keys are common in their graves; later law codes and sagas show women owning property and seeking divorce. Some were buried with great honour, like the two women laid in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oseberg-ship">Oseberg ship</a> in 834. A warrior’s grave at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birka">Birka</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, long assumed to be a man’s, was shown by DNA in 2017 to be a woman’s, though what this means is debated. Children worked on the farm from an early age, and fostering children in other households bound families together.</p><p>Trade grew alongside raiding. Market towns, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribe">Ribe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedeby">Hedeby</a> at the base of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a>, Birka on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-malaren">Lake Mälaren</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaupang">Kaupang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, drew craftsmen who made combs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a>, jewellery and weapons, and merchants who dealt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>, walrus <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, amber, slaves and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>. Silver was weighed rather than counted: coins, ingots and jewellery were cut into pieces and nicked to test their purity, and hoards of this hack-silver, rich in Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a>, have been found across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, above all on the island of Gotland. Kings learned to profit from trade and to protect it, as the Danish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudfred">Godfred</a> did when he strengthened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danevirke">Danevirke</a>, the earthwork across the base of Jutland, around 808.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Worldview</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/harald-bluetooth"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harald-bluetooth.jpg" alt="Harald Bluetooth" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/harald-bluetooth">Harald Bluetooth</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1200_Harald_Bl%C3%A5tand_anagoria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anagoria, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Vikings followed the Norse religion, which had no single church or creed. Cult was led by chieftains and kings, who held sacrificial feasts, blót, at their halls and at sacred places, and the gods were many, among them Odin, god of wisdom, poetry and the dead in battle, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thor">Thor</a>, the thunderer who guarded gods and people, and Freyr and Freyja, who gave fertility and plenty. Most of what is known of the myths was written down in Christian Iceland in the 13th century, in the poems of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edda">Poetic Edda</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snorri-sturluson">Snorri Sturluson</a>’s Prose Edda. Contemporary evidence is scarcer: runic inscriptions, place names, amulets such as Thor’s hammers, and the reports of outsiders. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-of-bremen">Adam of Bremen</a>, writing about 1075, described a temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a> with images of Thor, Odin and Freyr.</p><p>Burial customs varied from place to place: cremation and inhumation, in boats or under mounds, with weapons, tools, food, animals and sometimes slaves. The ship burials of Oseberg and Gokstad in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> are the richest known. The Arab traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-fadlan">Ibn Fadlan</a>, who met Rus’ merchants on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> in 922, described a chieftain burned in his ship after a slave girl had been killed to go with him.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> came slowly, through traders, missionaries and kings. The Frankish monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ansgar">Ansgar</a> preached at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birka">Birka</a> about 830. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-bluetooth">Harald Bluetooth</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, accepted baptism around 965 and raised the great stone at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jelling">Jelling</a>, which says that he “made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> Christian”. In Norway <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-tryggvason">Olaf Tryggvason</a>, king from 995 to 1000, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-ii-of-norway">Olaf Haraldsson</a>, killed at Stiklestad in 1030 and soon venerated as Saint Olaf, imposed the new faith by force; Iceland’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/althing">Althing</a> accepted it by agreement in 999 or 1000; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> converted more gradually, into the 12th century. The Vikings spoke Old Norse and wrote it in runes.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="arts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Arts &amp; Knowledge</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/urnes-stave-church"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/urnes-stave-church.jpg" alt="Urnes Stave Church" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/urnes-stave-church">Urnes Stave Church</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Urnes_Stave_Church_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Leo-setä, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Viking art is an art of ornament, above all of animals: gripping beasts, ribbon-like serpents and stylised creatures twist across wood, metal and stone. Art historians name a sequence of styles after find-places, Oseberg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borre-mound-cemetery">Borre</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jelling">Jelling</a>, Mammen, Ringerike and Urnes, running from about 800 into the 12th century, when the carved portal of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urnes-stave-church">Urnes Stave Church</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> shows the last of them. The Oseberg burial of 834 preserved the finest early woodwork: the ship’s spiralling prow, a carved cart, sledges and animal-head posts. Smiths made the paired oval brooches that held a woman’s dress, arm rings and the Mammen axe, inlaid with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-bluetooth">Harald Bluetooth</a>’s runestone at Jelling bears an early image of Christ.</p><p>The ships were the age’s great technical achievement. Built of overlapping oak planks on a keel, light and flexible, with a square sail and oars, they could cross open sea, run up shallow rivers and land on beaches. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokstad-ship">Gokstad ship</a> of about 890 is some 23 metres long, and a replica crossed the Atlantic in 1893. The Skuldelev ships, sunk as a barrier in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roskilde">Roskilde</a> Fjord in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and raised between 1957 and 1962, include a warship some 30 metres long built near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> about 1042 and a sturdy cargo ship from western Norway. Navigators steered without a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-compass">compass</a>, by the sun and stars, coastlines, birds and whales.</p><p>The Vikings wrote in the younger futhark, a runic alphabet of sixteen letters in use from about 800, cut into wood, bone and stone. Thousands of runestones, most raised in the late 10th and 11th centuries and most of them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, commemorate the dead and the journeys on which some died, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, in “Greece”, meaning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, or in Serkland, the Islamic lands. Poetry was the most admired art. Skalds composed praise poems for kings in intricate metres, rich in kennings, riddling poetic names for things; this verse, the mythological and heroic poems of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edda">Edda</a> and the prose sagas of kings and of Icelandic families were written down in Iceland between the 12th and 14th centuries.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="exchange">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Contact &amp; Exchange</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/varangian-guard"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/varangian-guard.jpg" alt="Varangian Guard" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/varangian-guard">Varangian Guard</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_body_of_Leo_V_is_dragged_to_the_Hippodrome_through_the_Skyla_Gate.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the west raids grew into conquest and settlement. Norse raiders built a fortified base at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> in 841, which grew into the chief Norse town and slave market of Ireland; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brian-boru">Brian Boru</a>’s victory at Clontarf in 1014 was later remembered as the end of Viking power there. Norse settlers took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shetland">Shetland</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a> and the Isle of Man. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-heathen-army">Great Heathen Army</a> landed in 865, took York, killed King Edmund of East Anglia in 869 and overran Northumbria, East Anglia and much of Mercia, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Kingdom of Wessex</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guthrum">Guthrum</a> at Edington in 878. The army’s followers settled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danelaw">Danelaw</a>, and a Viking kingdom at York lasted until 954. A second wave of attacks from the 980s brought heavy tributes, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danegeld">Danegeld</a>, and the conquest of England by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweyn-forkbeard">Sweyn Forkbeard</a> in 1013; his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut the Great</a> ruled England, the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and, from 1028, the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>.</p><p>On the Continent the Vikings rowed up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. They sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 845, and when they besieged it in 885–886 the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-fat">Charles the Fat</a> paid them to leave. In 911 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-simple">Charles the Simple</a> granted the leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rollo">Rollo</a> lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a>, the origin of the Duchy of Normandy. Fleets raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> in 844 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 859–861. In the North Atlantic, Norse farmers settled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroes</a>, Iceland from about 870 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a> from about 985 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erik-the-red">Erik the Red</a>, and briefly reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a>, where the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanse-aux-meadows">L’Anse aux Meadows</a>, found in 1960 and with wood cut there in 1021, confirms the voyages to Vinland told in the sagas.</p><p>In the east Scandinavians, mostly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, followed the rivers of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. They traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> and slaves for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate</a>, whose markets linked them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, and sailed down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, attacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 860 and later making trading treaties with it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> says that in 862 the Varangian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik">Rurik</a> was invited to rule at Novgorod; his successors moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus’</a>, whose rulers soon spoke Slavic. From about 988 Scandinavians served the Byzantine emperors in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangian-guard">Varangian Guard</a>, and a marble lion from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piraeus">Piraeus</a>, taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1687, still bears runes probably cut by such men.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rollo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rollo.jpg" alt="Rollo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rollo">Rollo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RolloA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AnonymousUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By the 11th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> was ending, as the Scandinavian kingdoms became Christian monarchies and royal war replaced private raiding. Its end is usually dated to 1066. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-hardrada">Harald Hardrada</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and a veteran of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangian-guard">Varangian Guard</a>, invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and was killed fighting the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold Godwinson</a> at Stamford Bridge on 25 September; less than three weeks later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William of Normandy</a>, a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rollo">Rollo</a>, defeated Harold at Hastings. Norse rule lingered at the edges: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a> and Man passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> in 1266, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shetland">Shetland</a> in 1468–1469, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a> settlements died out in the 15th century, their last written record a wedding at Hvalsey church in 1408.</p><p>The Vikings left their mark on language and land. English took from Old Norse everyday words such as sky, egg, knife, window, husband and law, and even the pronouns they, them and their, and hundreds of place names in northern and eastern England end in -by and -thorpe. Norn, a Norse language, was spoken in Orkney and Shetland until about the 18th century, and Icelandic remains close to Old Norse. The dukes of Normandy conquered England, other Normans won southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, and the Rurikid dynasty ruled the lands of the Rus’ until 1598.</p><p>Views of the Vikings have swung between extremes. Monastic chroniclers saw pagan destroyers. Nineteenth-century Romantics and nationalists made them heroic ancestors, and the horned helmets of popular imagination come from that age, notably from the costumes for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-wagner">Richard Wagner</a>’s Ring cycle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayreuth">Bayreuth</a> in 1876; the only nearly complete Viking helmet, found at Gjermundbu in Norway in 1943, has none. In 1962 Peter Sawyer argued that the chroniclers had exaggerated the raids, and excavations at York’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coppergate">Coppergate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>’s Wood Quay and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedeby">Hedeby</a> revealed busy towns of craftsmen and traders. Historians now hold both pictures together: the Vikings were slavers and killers as well as farmers, merchants, poets and founders of states.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Vikings were Scandinavian seafarers who between the late 8th and the 11th centuries raided, traded and settled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> to the Caspian. They plundered monasteries and towns, founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> and Normandy, ruled much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, settled Iceland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a> and gave rulers to the Rus’, while at home kingdoms, towns and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> took shape. Their words, place names and sagas endure.</em></p>

